1
00:00:05,724 --> 00:00:07,880
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,881 --> 00:00:12,360
This time, let's learn about various correction methods.

3
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,320
When and why do we use correction?

4
00:00:15,321 --> 00:00:21,000
When I'm stuck on a picture because I don't like it, I sometimes use the correction function.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:31,080
If you look at a picture for a long time while drawing, it becomes so familiar that it's hard to figure out whether there are problems or whether there's a better direction.

6
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:37,020
So I tell people to look at the picture again the next day, next week, next month.

7
00:00:37,021 --> 00:00:43,301
When you look at the picture again when it's somewhat forgotten, you can view it from a more objective perspective.

8
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:47,760
You can see a similar effect when you do correction.

9
00:00:47,761 --> 00:00:51,920
Because the feel of the picture changes noticeably depending on the degree of correction.

10
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:59,841
I think this correction function is where the advantage of digital painting is maximized.

11
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:08,190
Correction is more effective in illustration than concept art, but it can be applied to concept art too, so let's study it.

12
00:01:09,614 --> 00:01:13,560
First, let's learn about the area selection method for correction.

13
00:01:13,561 --> 00:01:19,521
When you want to change a specific part or color, it's convenient if the layers are divided.

14
00:01:19,669 --> 00:01:24,440
Using the layer functions we learned in the shortcut explanation time, you can easily correct it.

15
00:01:24,441 --> 00:01:28,440
But what if the layers are merged into one like this?

16
00:01:28,441 --> 00:01:31,640
If you want to select only a specific area within a layer.

17
00:01:31,641 --> 00:01:34,680
You can use various area selection tools.

18
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:46,240
You can use the lasso tool or the magic wand tool I mentioned earlier.

19
00:01:46,241 --> 00:01:51,360
But the method I often use is this color range selection function.

20
00:01:52,959 --> 00:01:57,150
If you open the color range window, it changes to an eyedropper-shaped cursor like this.

21
00:01:58,359 --> 00:02:02,450
Click the color you want to select in the picture.

22
00:02:06,454 --> 00:02:12,960
Here, adjusting the tolerance lets you set how similar colors to the one you selected a moment ago are also selected.

23
00:02:14,359 --> 00:02:21,411
For the range, you can set the range of selection based on the position you clicked a moment ago.

24
00:02:23,795 --> 00:02:28,380
Changing the selection area preview like this makes it easier to view.

25
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,930
There are pros and cons to this.

26
00:02:33,931 --> 00:02:38,650
First, to talk about the downside: it's hard to select only the area you want.

27
00:02:38,651 --> 00:02:42,090
Because it pulls in all colors of a similar family.

28
00:02:42,091 --> 00:02:45,971
You can adjust it somewhat with the tolerance.

29
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:52,661
The advantage is that, since it selects all similar colors, you can correct while maintaining unity.

30
00:02:53,480 --> 00:03:01,000
After selecting like this, you duplicate the selection area, then do the correction.

31
00:03:01,001 --> 00:03:02,360
You keep the original.

32
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:09,660
Let me do a correction as an example.

33
00:03:09,661 --> 00:03:12,060
Only the selected area can be corrected like this.

34
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:19,980
After selecting like this, you can correct in various ways.

35
00:03:20,225 --> 00:03:23,621
Let me go through the corrections I often use, one by one.

36
00:03:27,825 --> 00:03:32,490
First, let's closely look at the curve correction I've used often in previous chapters.

37
00:03:33,505 --> 00:03:36,330
Let's first look at the graph.

38
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:39,290
Here there's a part labeled input.

39
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:48,410
This input axis represents the brightness and darkness of the original state, and this output axis represents the brightness and darkness after correction.

40
00:03:48,411 --> 00:04:06,601
For example, assuming the axis range is 0 to 255, if you raise the middle part like this, it means the brightness of the part corresponding to 127 in the input appears changed to 196 in the output.

41
00:04:06,964 --> 00:04:13,360
I only raised the middle part here, but you can see the other parts come up along with it.

42
00:04:13,361 --> 00:04:18,160
In this way, this graph is always transformed into the shape of a curve, so it's called curve correction.

43
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:25,900
And these bar graphs represent the value distribution of the picture.

44
00:04:26,015 --> 00:04:33,141
This picture is mostly midtones overall, so the graphs are centered like this.

45
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:37,640
Let me also look at it with another picture.

46
00:04:38,205 --> 00:04:42,321
This picture's contrast is distributed on the high side overall.

47
00:04:43,575 --> 00:04:46,880
Here, there are relatively many midtones and dark tones.

48
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:52,480
That's because the scales, which take up a large area here, are in the midtones and dark tones.

49
00:04:54,245 --> 00:04:59,280
And I don't touch these two endpoints much.

50
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:04,480
Forcibly unifying the value loses a lot of information.

51
00:05:04,481 --> 00:05:08,240
For example, after raising this left endpoint up.

52
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:17,761
If you press OK and turn the curve correction on again, you can see that all the information in the dark area on this left side is lost.

53
00:05:19,710 --> 00:05:23,280
However, I sometimes use it when drawing backgrounds.

54
00:05:23,281 --> 00:05:33,920
To give a sense of atmospheric perspective, I remove the darkest area so the darkest part of the background doesn't get darker than the darkest part of the character.

55
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:48,860
In this way, you can correct RGB all at once, or you can correct them separately.

56
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:56,581
For example, if I leave it on red and do curve correction, I can only adjust the red tone.

57
00:06:01,070 --> 00:06:05,861
If I raise only the middle part, only the midtones become red.

58
00:06:19,585 --> 00:06:24,300
For this curve correction, I usually use an S-curve correction.

59
00:06:24,301 --> 00:06:33,221
You can keep the midtones and make the bright tones brighter and the dark parts darker, to raise the contrast.

60
00:06:38,415 --> 00:06:41,400
A correction like raising the middle like a mountain, like this.

61
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:46,200
I sometimes use that too.

62
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:51,400
Keeping the overall contrast but bringing out the midtones so the picture has stability.

63
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:17,191
Besides this, there are various value correction methods like brightness and contrast, and levels, but I recommend curve correction, which is intuitive and can make detailed corrections.

64
00:07:21,335 --> 00:07:24,000
Next is hue and saturation correction.

65
00:07:24,001 --> 00:07:26,080
The shortcut is Control and U.

66
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:36,281
When you open this correction window, the familiar HSV sliders appear, and with these you correct the layer or the selected area within the layer.

67
00:07:37,150 --> 00:07:41,120
If you move the hue slider, it corrects based on the current hue.

68
00:07:43,525 --> 00:07:47,561
For example, this red line, if you move it to the right.

69
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:54,821
It changes to yellow, green, and then blue in order.

70
00:07:55,825 --> 00:08:02,531
If you check the colorize button here and try it, it changes based on the hue in the slider.

71
00:08:03,710 --> 00:08:07,241
It's good to use it together with the area selection function we learned before.

72
00:08:08,305 --> 00:08:10,410
Shall we try it?

73
00:08:10,411 --> 00:08:13,571
Let me change this red color to a different color.

74
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,810
Open the color range window, click this part.

75
00:08:21,190 --> 00:08:24,140
Adjust the tolerance, and press OK.

76
00:08:25,495 --> 00:08:31,120
Duplicate the layer, press Control and U, and adjust the slider.

77
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:38,169
You can easily change the color, right?

78
00:08:38,170 --> 00:08:42,770
Since I don't want to change the skin color, I'll erase it.

79
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:52,030
I also sometimes use the color balance function.

80
00:08:52,031 --> 00:08:53,310
It's Control and B.

81
00:08:54,755 --> 00:08:59,390
You can correct the highlights, midtones, and shadows separately.

82
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:01,230
Shall we try it?

83
00:09:01,359 --> 00:09:07,950
If you click the highlights like this and move the slider, it corrects mainly the bright parts.

84
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:13,550
Conversely, if you click the shadows and try it, it corrects mainly the dark areas, right?

85
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:25,281
This can be used effectively on the pictures with the lighting setup I said I use often: warm key light, cool fill light, or reflected light.

86
00:09:26,030 --> 00:09:38,791
Move the slider toward warm colors like yellow and red for the bright areas, and toward cool colors like cyan and blue for the dark areas.

87
00:09:39,710 --> 00:09:49,881
If you do that, the bright parts, that is, the parts receiving the key light, get their warm tone alive, and the dark parts receive the blue reflected light.

88
00:09:59,719 --> 00:10:00,720
And the cool tone comes alive.

89
00:10:00,721 --> 00:10:03,040
Next is the color lookup function.

90
00:10:03,041 --> 00:10:07,241
There's no shortcut, so you click to use it.

91
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:11,770
I don't know why it's called the color lookup function.

92
00:10:11,771 --> 00:10:16,410
It's a function I learned when I worked on Riot's splash art.

93
00:10:16,411 --> 00:10:18,970
A lot of people use photo filter apps, right?

94
00:10:18,971 --> 00:10:21,690
You can think of it as a similar function.

95
00:10:21,691 --> 00:10:24,931
It's better for illustration work than character key art.

96
00:10:26,710 --> 00:10:31,760
Anyway, if you look at the top item here, there are various presets.

97
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:34,260
Let me change it.

98
00:10:36,275 --> 00:10:37,900
There are various feels.

99
00:10:48,515 --> 00:10:51,390
I'm a bit drawn to this feel.

100
00:10:51,391 --> 00:10:56,590
But using it as-is is too much and clumsy.

101
00:10:56,591 --> 00:10:59,431
When that happens, there's a function we can use.

102
00:11:03,735 --> 00:11:06,560
That's turning it into a correction layer.

103
00:11:06,561 --> 00:11:15,040
If you click the circle button in the layers panel here, there's the same color lookup button.

104
00:11:15,041 --> 00:11:16,041
Click this.

105
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:23,230
If you select the same preset, you can see it changes.

106
00:11:27,420 --> 00:11:31,111
Since it's been turned into a layer, through opacity adjustment.

107
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:36,090
You can give a difference in degree.

108
00:11:36,091 --> 00:11:37,850
So natural correction is possible.

109
00:11:39,055 --> 00:11:43,610
Since you can also do masking, even more natural correction becomes possible.

110
00:11:43,611 --> 00:11:52,860
If you look at the square part of this layers panel, it's all white, which means the correction property of this layer is applied to all areas.

111
00:11:54,270 --> 00:12:02,560
If you activate the brush, select black or a gray of your desired density from the color picker, and paint.

112
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:12,951
You can see the darkened part is excluded from the correction.

113
00:12:22,885 --> 00:12:26,601
Conversely, the whitened part is re-applied with the correction.

114
00:12:27,475 --> 00:12:29,580
Using an airbrush would be natural, right?

115
00:12:29,581 --> 00:12:34,980
Other corrections can likewise be turned into layers.

116
00:12:46,755 --> 00:12:54,270
Here, if you hold Alt and hover the cursor between the correction layer and a layer, this icon appears.

117
00:12:54,670 --> 00:12:58,990
If you click at that time, the correction is applied only to that layer below.

118
00:13:00,195 --> 00:13:03,950
Let me try it with another picture.

119
00:13:04,030 --> 00:13:11,870
If I want to apply this correction layer only to this layer below, hold Alt and click between the layers.

120
00:13:16,670 --> 00:13:20,840
Group the layers, and likewise hold Alt.

121
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:29,550
If you click between the correction layer and the group file, the correction is applied only to the grouped layers.

122
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:36,890
I hope you'll use these functions well and operate the correction functions smartly.

123
00:13:38,565 --> 00:13:43,200
Besides these Photoshop correction functions, there are also ways to add light with a brush, and so on.

124
00:13:46,085 --> 00:13:50,640
You paint using brushes like an airbrush.

125
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,400
You can use a standard layer like this.

126
00:13:53,695 --> 00:13:58,300
Or you can use various layer properties to give a light feeling.

127
00:13:59,830 --> 00:14:02,920
Naturally, you'd paint the parts that should shine.

128
00:14:02,921 --> 00:14:06,241
This also isn't good if you paint it just anywhere like this.

129
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:10,771
It's good to use it only on the parts you want to emphasize.

130
00:14:20,910 --> 00:14:28,910
Like this.

131
00:14:28,911 --> 00:14:30,990
How should you do correction?

132
00:14:31,070 --> 00:14:36,951
The purpose of these correction functions is to bring out the points of the picture you've expressed.

133
00:14:37,715 --> 00:14:45,020
If this high-density metal is the point, it's good to correct it so this part stands out better and looks shiny.

134
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:51,530
And you can also try changing this intense red to be more vivid.

135
00:14:54,810 --> 00:14:58,220
Then let me organize the purpose of correction.

136
00:14:58,221 --> 00:15:02,181
One, for clarity and gaze concentration on the part that will be the point.

137
00:15:02,735 --> 00:15:06,780
Two, to separate object from object.

138
00:15:06,781 --> 00:15:08,860
Three, for varied colors.

139
00:15:11,770 --> 00:15:21,500
For number one, gaze concentration on the part that will be the point, the number one correction is an extension of the concentrate and release work.

140
00:15:21,501 --> 00:15:26,220
Naturally, you'd want to make the concentrated part stand out.

141
00:15:26,221 --> 00:15:30,940
Usually, I make one layer and add light with an airbrush, doing a lot of that work.

142
00:15:32,145 --> 00:15:35,450
It's especially effective on these metals.

143
00:15:35,451 --> 00:15:39,450
If the light is strong, you can also do this correction on the skin.

144
00:15:39,451 --> 00:15:42,730
Also, the atmosphere reflects light too.

145
00:15:42,731 --> 00:15:45,370
Usually, on the part you want to emphasize.

146
00:15:46,805 --> 00:15:49,470
I design it so light reaches it.

147
00:15:49,471 --> 00:15:52,871
If you broadly apply it with an airbrush on the side the light comes from, like this.

148
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:59,971
You can get the effect of making the light feeling rich.

149
00:16:01,210 --> 00:16:05,540
You can even more bring out the part you want to emphasize.

150
00:16:08,415 --> 00:16:11,891
I use an airbrush for these tasks too.

151
00:16:13,085 --> 00:16:17,011
But I mainly use this soft texture brush here.

152
00:16:18,915 --> 00:16:24,030
Because with an airbrush, the brush mark doesn't remain, so it can be a bit boring at times.

153
00:16:30,170 --> 00:16:38,460
For number two, separating object from object. This is also one of the parts I consider important when drawing.

154
00:16:38,461 --> 00:16:58,810
On a flat canvas, the overlap between objects like this can be an element that somewhat reduces the three-dimensionality.

155
00:16:58,811 --> 00:17:05,690
I draw to avoid overlapping as much as possible, but obsessively avoiding overlap when placing them is also strange.

156
00:17:05,691 --> 00:17:09,690
I mean, a character can't be keeping their arms spread out.

157
00:17:09,691 --> 00:17:11,450
In cases where the arm overlaps like this, I do this.

158
00:17:12,229 --> 00:17:13,670
I separate the layer of the arm.

159
00:17:18,139 --> 00:17:21,230
And add light to the below naturally.

160
00:17:32,080 --> 00:17:40,080
This part here is the same too.

161
00:17:40,081 --> 00:17:47,801
Even though it's a black cape, using a blue mist, I took action so the body's silhouette comes alive.

162
00:17:48,784 --> 00:17:57,130
The overlapping part of the weapon and the hat above it was likewise painted with a blue airbrush to correct it.

163
00:17:57,131 --> 00:17:59,050
The separation between objects is important.

164
00:17:59,699 --> 00:18:04,090
You can do it with design, or with posing.

165
00:18:04,091 --> 00:18:09,610
If objects unavoidably overlap, you have to separate them with post-correction like this.

166
00:18:14,144 --> 00:18:21,141
For number three, varied colors. This is what I think is Photoshop's most amazing ability.

167
00:18:22,294 --> 00:18:34,400
Even if I habitually draw using only this area here, by using the correction function I can create color values in areas I didn't use.

168
00:18:34,401 --> 00:18:37,040
I can easily create colors I didn't have.

169
00:18:38,639 --> 00:18:44,530
Imagine you discover a piece of clothing with a design you really like at a clothing store.

170
00:18:44,531 --> 00:18:47,810
But the color you like is discontinued.

171
00:18:47,811 --> 00:18:49,890
You'd turn around.

172
00:18:49,891 --> 00:18:52,050
You also try dyeing at the hair salon.

173
00:18:52,051 --> 00:18:57,570
I bleached multiple times and dyed it, but I didn't get the color I wanted.

174
00:18:57,571 --> 00:19:01,890
By the time the hair was as damaged as it was going to get, they told me I couldn't re-dye it.

175
00:19:01,891 --> 00:19:07,570
But in digital drawing, you can freely change colors for free, without limit.

176
00:19:08,514 --> 00:19:10,220
Until you get the color you want.

177
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:15,100
Whether it'll be pretty to other people is a question after that.

178
00:19:16,484 --> 00:19:21,040
Just because I dye my hair like a celebrity doesn't mean I'll become as cool as a celebrity.

179
00:19:21,734 --> 00:19:23,920
So the base is important.

180
00:19:23,921 --> 00:19:30,304
That's the reason I tell beginners to refrain from using the correction function when doing practice or copy-study.

181
00:19:31,855 --> 00:19:35,960
Now let's look at a real-world example.

182
00:19:35,961 --> 00:19:38,920
What kind of correction did this picture get?

183
00:19:38,921 --> 00:19:42,161
In illustration, the family of colors is important, I said.

184
00:19:42,629 --> 00:19:52,280
This picture unifies this blue tone, and does a correction that separates only the point-colored skin and the purple marbles.

185
00:19:52,281 --> 00:19:59,281
As a result, the overall tone is unified and the point elements could stand out more.

186
00:20:00,534 --> 00:20:08,880
This picture uses backlight, and I wanted to create a very strong highlight through the effect of total internal reflection.

187
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:11,760
Especially on this hair and shoulder area.

188
00:20:11,761 --> 00:20:15,690
As a result, I could concentrate the gaze toward the face.

189
00:20:18,354 --> 00:20:22,460
And this picture wasn't originally planned with this pink tint from the start.

190
00:20:23,794 --> 00:20:26,700
Originally, since it's a gumiho, I dressed it in white traditional clothes.

191
00:20:27,964 --> 00:20:33,800
And receiving yellow moonlight, it was a picture that was overall yellow and bright in feeling.

192
00:20:33,801 --> 00:20:42,600
Since the fox was white, the clothes white, the skin white, and the hair white, the picture really looked boring.

193
00:20:42,601 --> 00:20:45,800
The picture from that time isn't saved, so I'm sorry I can't show it to you.

194
00:20:45,801 --> 00:20:51,720
Anyway, thinking like that, I couldn't go further, and I started touching the correction function.

195
00:20:51,721 --> 00:20:56,680
As I fiddled with the correction here and there, the colors became varied, and I began to see a path.

196
00:20:57,909 --> 00:20:59,960
Then I drew a few more days and finished it.

197
00:21:00,709 --> 00:21:04,760
In this way, the correction function can also be used in the middle stage of a picture.

198
00:21:06,144 --> 00:21:14,350
As a side note, this picture is a picture I drew when I was twenty-four, and after nine years, I've retouched it a bit now.

199
00:21:16,194 --> 00:21:22,151
As you can see, I balanced the left and right with a transform.

200
00:21:23,830 --> 00:21:26,680
And slightly improved the clarity.

201
00:21:26,681 --> 00:21:30,520
When I was twenty-four, I drew without glasses.

202
00:21:30,521 --> 00:21:34,200
It's fun to see the difference caused by glasses.

203
00:21:34,201 --> 00:21:37,521
And I said you should bring out the point elements even more.

204
00:21:37,719 --> 00:21:44,321
Through the color correction explained this time, I made the overall pink tone stand out even more.

205
00:21:45,340 --> 00:21:50,830
Through various modifications and corrections, I think the picture became a bit more stable.

206
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:59,260
What do you think?

207
00:21:59,261 --> 00:22:02,620
Then how can this picture be corrected?

208
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:09,980
Because I focused on drawing elements I hadn't drawn often, the planning wasn't thorough, so I don't like it.

209
00:22:09,981 --> 00:22:13,221
I should at least use correction to bring the picture to life a bit.

210
00:22:14,484 --> 00:22:19,110
If it were me, here, to bring out the point panels.

211
00:22:30,999 --> 00:22:32,700
Add light and raise the background contrast.

212
00:22:58,819 --> 00:22:59,820
And separate it from the character.

213
00:22:59,821 --> 00:23:04,261
And bring out the blue tone of the darkness and shadows.

214
00:23:06,330 --> 00:23:10,501
And mix a bit of warm color into the light-receiving side.

215
00:23:12,674 --> 00:23:17,790
So I could add interest to the uniform white tone that could look boring.

216
00:24:07,479 --> 00:24:11,530
We've learned about various correction functions and methods.

217
00:24:11,531 --> 00:24:18,531
These correction functions are like seasoning that makes a well-drawn picture look even better.

218
00:24:20,574 --> 00:24:28,211
As commercial artists, we need to make our pictures sell a bit better.

219
00:24:29,444 --> 00:24:35,561
If you're an active illustrator, you should work hard to get even one more like.

220
00:24:36,975 --> 00:24:42,760
Correction functions play the role of turning an eighty-point picture into an eighty-two.

221
00:24:42,761 --> 00:24:48,440
But used wrongly, an eighty-point picture could become a seventy.

222
00:24:48,441 --> 00:24:52,600
Also, correcting a fifty-point picture can't turn it into a ninety.

223
00:24:53,879 --> 00:24:58,810
The purpose of correction is to make a picture a bit more pleasing.

224
00:24:58,811 --> 00:25:01,210
The base has to be good for correction to shine too.

225
00:25:02,459 --> 00:25:06,990
I compared the correction function to black bean sauce over fried rice.

226
00:25:06,991 --> 00:25:10,231
These days, it's hard to find really delicious fried rice.

227
00:25:10,429 --> 00:25:12,350
It's called oil rice.

228
00:25:12,350 --> 00:25:14,590
Since I like Chinese food, I eat it often.

229
00:25:15,364 --> 00:25:22,910
It's a fried rice of oil-soaked rice topped with black bean sauce, sold for nine thousand won, and it really feels like betrayal sometimes.

230
00:25:22,911 --> 00:25:30,270
It's less of a cost for ingredients and quick to make, which is good, but you can't build a regular customer.

231
00:25:30,271 --> 00:25:33,010
Really well-made fried rice is delicious even just eating the rice.

232
00:25:34,354 --> 00:25:40,741
I should work hard to draw the base picture beautifully, without relying on the correction function.

233
00:25:42,684 --> 00:25:47,960
This time, we talked about various correction methods and how to use them.

234
00:25:47,960 --> 00:25:52,280
Next time, let's talk about using various Photoshop functions.
